## 1. Rule-(a) exclusion correctness

anomalies.go:169-178. `groups := merchants.GroupTransactions(expenses)` where
`expenses` is already Active()-filtered and Outflow/Amount<0-filtered
(anomalies.go:157-167) — groups can only ever contain qualifying rows, so a
group cannot "qualify" via mixed-sign or suppressed rows sneaking in; the
filter is airtight (also directly regression-tested by
TestDetect_ExpenseFilter_ExcludesNonOutflowOrPositiveAmount, which plants a
positive-amount Outflow row and a negative-amount Income row inside an
otherwise-qualifying merchant group and asserts neither the rows themselves
nor the group's baseline members get flagged).

`categoryPool` (line 173-178) is built by taking only groups with
`len(group) < minMerchantGroupRows`; qualifying groups (n>=4) are excluded
from the category median/MAD population entirely — not just from category
scoring, from the pool itself. Meanwhile the `mad_merchant` loop (line
213-226) iterates the FULL `groups` map (not `categoryPool`), so a
qualifying group's own outliers are still tested — "judged within that
group only," exactly as specced. Confirmed by
TestDetect_PlantedFixture_DominantConstantChargeDoesNotCollapseCategoryMAD
(SPOTIFY n=78 / NETFLIX n=18 excluded from Subscriptions baseline, 8 noise
rows correctly unflagged) and TestDetect_SmallGroup_MaterialOutlierFlagged
(n=4 group's own $200 outlier correctly flagged via mad_merchant).

## 2. Percentile/median/MAD correctness

median() (line 338-350): standard even-length averaging, odd-length middle
element, 0 for empty — unit-tested (TestMedian) including the even case.
percentile() (line 365-383): numpy "linear" interpolation, matches
TestPercentile's hand-computed 95.5 for a 10-element vector. MAD is
median(|v-med|) (line 353-359), correctly a single median call, not
recomputed sort-of-abs-values incorrectly.

Division-by-zero / MAD=0-and-median=0: the expense filter is
TransactionType==Outflow AND Amount<0 (line 161), so every value entering
absAmounts/median/MAD is strictly positive; median of a set of strictly
positive numbers is always >0, so the "MAD==0 AND median==0" case is
provably unreachable, not just empirically so — confirmed by re-reading the
filter (line 161) and the fact that Detect never calls median/MAD/percentile
on anything except `absAmounts` of `expenses`-derived slices. The comment at
line 282-284 admits this ("kept explicit for safety") — accurate
self-assessment, not overclaiming. Filter is airtight; no seam I could find
lets a zero or non-Outflow row into a baseline (also directly
regression-tested by TestDetect_ExpenseFilter_ExcludesNonOutflowOrPositiveAmount).

Single-element MAD (used only via the standalone unit-testable functions,
never in Detect's live path since minMerchantGroupRows=4 and
minCategoryRows=6 gate both MAD call sites) has no special-cased panic risk
— median() on len-1 returns the element, MAD then reduces to 0, handled by
the MAD==0 fallback branch.

## 3. new_merchant semantics on this data model

Confirmed: p95 is computed on the SAME `expenses` slice the groups are
derived from (line 229: `percentile(absAmounts(expenses), ...)`) — same
window, no skew between the group population and the percentile population.
new_merchant runs over "every merchant group (any size)" (line 230,
explicitly documented at line 34) — a group needs only >=1 occurrence, no
minimum-n gate, unlike the two MAD methods. A transaction CAN be both its
group's first occurrence and a mad_category candidate simultaneously (e.g. a
singleton merchant's amount also clears its category's z-threshold);
dedupe's `candidates` map keyed by Hash with `score > existing.Score`
(line 193) correctly keeps only the higher-scoring method's Anomaly — this
exact scenario (dual mad_merchant + new_merchant hit on one transaction) is
directly regression-tested by TestDetect_Dedupe_HigherScoreMethodWins.

The "narrow window makes everything new" consequence IS documented — package
doc lines 13-16 state Detect "has no notion of 'recent' or 'this month'"
and the caller controls the window entirely via pre-filtering (e.g.
FilterByDateRange, confirmed present at internal/models/transaction.go:133).
This matches the b2 v1 Ruling that accepted the behavior as
consistent/documented rather than parameterizing it away.

FP-rate quantification (FINDING, not a failure — see §4 below): by
construction, whatever the window, exactly the top (100-95)=5% of expense
rows by absolute amount clear the p95 gate. The material question is what
fraction of THAT top-5% is also "first occurrence of its merchant group in
the window" — the intersection is not fixed by the algorithm and depends
entirely on the real merchant-frequency distribution the caller feeds in.
Two distinct real-data risk patterns, both worth flagging for P4 (handler
design), neither a P2 defect since P2 correctly implements the ruled,
documented, window-relative definition:
  (i) Long-tail one-off big-ticket purchases (auto repair, one-time medical
      bills, appliances, travel) are disproportionately represented in the
      amount tail AND are frequently genuine one-time merchants within any
      realistic window — so a non-trivial share of the 5% p95-tail will
      also be first-occurrence, independent of window width. This is the
      "expected" new_merchant use case and is benign.
  (ii) The sharper risk: for a NARROW window (e.g. a 1-month insights page),
      any bill that recurs only once per window by its own cadence (rent,
      mortgage, an annual/semi-annual insurance premium sliced into a
      1-month view) is, by definition, its own group's sole/first member in
      that window — with no n>=4 gate protecting it (unlike the MAD
      methods). If that bill is also one of the largest line items in the
      month (common for rent/mortgage), it clears p95 essentially every
      month, producing a CHRONIC repeat false positive on the user's
      best-known, least-anomalous expense — worse than a one-off tail FP
      because it repeats indefinitely rather than being a rare event. The
      current test suite does not exercise this scenario (the long-standing-
      group test, TestDetect_NewMerchant_LongStandingGroupNeverFlagged, uses
      an 18-month unfiltered set where NETFLIX's first occurrence sits at
      the very start and is small-amount, so it never probes a monthly
      once-per-window recurring bill against a p95 computed on ONLY that
      month). Recommend P4 either compute new_merchant's first-occurrence
      test against a materially wider lookback than the display window, or
      add a minimum-occurrences-ever guard to new_merchant specifically.
      Ruled-parameter consequence per the task's framing, so it does not
      fail P2, but severity is HIGH for UX impact and should be carried
      forward explicitly to P4/handler design, not left implicit.

## 4. Fixture honesty judgment

fixture_test.go:226-241 documents, in its own comments, that the 5
category-anomaly baselines were deliberately split into merchant groups of
size 3/3/2/2 with ascending dates chosen so each group's EARLIEST (hence
new_merchant-candidate) member is always its lowest-jitter (at-or-below-
median) row — explicitly to guarantee "no baseline row's amount is ever a
new_merchant candidate, regardless of where the global 95th percentile
happens to fall."

Judgment: this is LEGITIMATE fixture design, not a fixture that hides a
real defect. The stated purpose of buildPlantedFixture is to isolate and
verify the mad_category/mad_merchant/rule-(a)/rule-(b)/rule-(c) machinery
with a known, hand-computed ground truth (5 exact planted anomalies, 5.5x
median, tight baselines) — cross-contaminating that fixture with incidental
new_merchant noise would make the FlagsFiveWithAtMostOneFalsePositive
assertion untestable (an accidental new_merchant hit on a baseline row would
either eat the 1-FP budget for an unrelated reason or force loosening the
bound), without adding any coverage of new_merchant itself, which is
separately and adequately covered by
TestDetect_NewMerchant_FirstSeenAbovePercentileFlagged and
TestDetect_NewMerchant_LongStandingGroupNeverFlagged. Planted truths remain
honest: the comment is explicit and correct about what was engineered and
why, nothing is misrepresented, and no assertion in the test suite claims
to bound real-world new_merchant FP rates — the ≤1 FP bound is scoped to
this fixture only. The gap is that no test in this suite quantifies or
bounds new_merchant's FP rate under a realistic merchant-frequency
distribution (see §3(ii) above) — that is a coverage gap worth carrying to
P4, not a dishonesty problem in this fixture.

## 5. Determinism and sorting

Final sort (line 252-257): Score descending, ties broken by Hash ascending
— fully deterministic regardless of any map iteration order upstream.
Verified via TestDetect_Determinism (two Detect() calls compared with
reflect.DeepEqual) and TestDetect_ResultsSortedByScoreDescending, both
passing under -race -count=2 (fresh map instantiation each run, so Go's
per-map iteration randomization is actually exercised across the two runs,
not just theoretically possible).

One narrow, low-severity latent seam: the dedupe step's per-Hash winner
(line 193, `score > existing.Score`, strict inequality) resolves an EXACT
score tie between two different methods on the SAME transaction by "whichever
was considered first," which does depend on `groups` map iteration order
(mad_merchant and new_merchant both range over the same `groups` map,
built fresh each Detect() call). This cannot affect final ordering between
different transactions (Hash tie-break in the sort is order-independent),
only which Method/PeerGroup/MadZ is reported for a transaction whose two
methods happen to produce bit-identical float scores. This is
astronomically unlikely with real dollar amounts (a MAD z-score and a
percentile ratio matching to full float64 precision) and none of the
existing tests hit it. Not a material defect on plausible data; noting for
completeness per the task's explicit ask to check map-order dependence.

## Overall

The Go port faithfully realizes the ruled §2 standard: rule (a)/(b)/(c),
the MAD==0 fallback, minima, materiality floor, high-side-only, dedupe,
severity, and sort/tie-break all match the spec text and are each covered
by a targeted regression test tracing to a specific b2-build Ruling
(dominant-constant-charge, immaterial wobble, low-side outlier). The
expense filter (Outflow AND Amount<0) is airtight and correctly makes the
MAD=0-and-median=0 degenerate case unreachable. merchants.GroupTransactions
is used correctly (DisplayName-then-Description precedence matches
anomalies.go's own displayNameOrDescription helper) and its already-accepted
subset-merge/degenerate-guard logic (P1) is not re-litigated here. No
material defect found on plausible data. The one thing I'd escalate as a
FINDING (not a FAIL) for the lead going into P4: new_merchant has no
minimum-occurrence gate and is window-relative by design, which is a ruled
and documented consequence, but on narrow windows it can chronically
re-flag large once-per-window recurring bills (rent/mortgage-class
expenses) — a UX severity concern the current fixture, by deliberate and
honestly-documented design, does not exercise or quantify.

PASS.
